both sides by 2:\n\n\\[\nn(n+1) = 132\n\\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation:\n\n\\[\nn^2 + n - 132 = 0\n\\]\n\nSolving using the quadratic formula \\(n = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^ thec}}{2a}\\) where \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = 1\\), and \\(c = -132\\):\n\n\\[\nn = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{1 + 4 \\times 132}}{2} = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{529}}{2} = \\frac{-1 \\pm 23}{2}\n\\]\n\nTaking the positive root:\n\n\\[\nn = \\frac{22}{2} = 11\n\\]\n\nThus, 66 is the 11th triangular number. The next term in the sequence (the 12th triangular number) is calculated by:\n\n\\[\nT_{12} = \\frac{12 \\times 13}{2} = \\frac{156}{2} = 78\n\\]\n\nWe can also verify this by adding the next integer to the previous triangular number:\n\n\\[\n66 + 12 = 78\n\\]\n\nListing the triangular numbers up to the 13th term confirms the pattern:\n- \\(T_1 = 1\\)\n- \\(T_2 = ",
